/-
# Signed evaluation of the finite near core by the target-gap split

`NearCoreDeterminantTaper` reduces the surviving off-ordinate energy of the three-taper
observer to the single scalar

    ∫ q(u) · S(t, J; u) du ,
    S(t, J; u) = ∑_{σ ∈ nearOffFinset t J} m_σ · cosh(a_σ u) · cos((b_σ − t) u) ,

`q = detTaper n₁ n₂ g r` the determinant taper, `a_σ = Re σ − 1/2` the height and
`b_σ − t` the *target gap* of the near zero `σ`.

`NearCoreNarrowWindowNoCancellation` settles the case `J·Λ ≤ π/2`: there every phase
`(b_σ − t)u` stays inside a quarter period, all cosines are nonnegative and the core is
pinned between two positive multiples of the near mass.  The only escape it leaves open
is a cutoff exceeding the quarter period, `π/2 < J·Λ`, where some target gaps really do
oscillate against the taper.

This module evaluates the core in **that** regime, and it does so by transporting an
estimate the repository already owns rather than by inventing one.  `OscillatoryKernelDecay`
proves, for a compactly supported `C²` weight `w`,

    |∫ w(u) cos(δ u) du| ≤ ‖w''‖₁ / δ²                     (`abs_integral_mul_cos_le`)

by two exact integrations by parts.  Its problem is the same problem as the near core's
once the weight is identified with the literal taper–height profile
`w = q · cosh(a·)`; `zeroDetResponse_eq_integral_detWeight_mul_cos` is that identity, on
the nose, for the same `q`, the same height `a_σ`, the same gap `b_σ − t` and the same
integration semantics.  So the donor estimate applies to each *individual* near zero:

* `abs_zeroDetResponse_le` — `|zeroDetResponse … a δ| ≤ detWeightCurvature … a / δ²`;
* `zeroDetResponse_ge_of_gap_le` — the complementary pin: if `|δ| ≤ D` and `D·Λ ≤ π/2`
  then `zeroDetResponse … a δ ≥ cos(DΛ) · ∫ q`, whatever the cutoff `J` is.

Splitting the near carrier at an arbitrary gap threshold `D` therefore yields a genuine
two-sided *signed* evaluation of the finite near core, with no restriction on `J`:

    lowGapMass(t,J,D) · cos(DΛ) · ∫ q  −  highGapMass(t,J,D) · C / D²
        ≤ ∫ q · S(t,J)                                    (`integral_taper_phaseSum_ge_gap_split`)

where `C` bounds the curvature `‖(q·cosh(a_σ ·))''‖₁` of the near zeros.  Two
consequences are recorded:

* `highGapMass_pos_of_small_core` — *cancellation can only be produced by zeros whose
  target gap exceeds the quarter-period threshold*.  If the core drops below the
  low-gap pin, the high-gap mass is strictly positive; the low-gap zeros contribute with
  a fixed sign and cannot cancel anything.
* `not_scalar_bound_of_gap_split` — whenever the split lower bound already reaches the
  consumer threshold, the scalar hypothesis of the near-core consumer is unsatisfiable at
  that taper, cutoff and threshold.  This closes the `π/2 < J·Λ` escape quantitatively:
  it survives only while `highGapMass · C / D²` exceeds the low-gap pin.

Nothing here asserts or assumes the Riemann Hypothesis, and nothing weakens
`RiemannHypothesisFor`.  No `sorry`, no `axiom`, no Boolean receipt, no numerical
evidence.
-/
import Zeta23Bridge.NearCoreNarrowWindowNoCancellation
import Zeta23Bridge.OscillatoryKernelDecay

noncomputable section

open scoped Real
open Zeta23 MeasureTheory

namespace Zeta23Bridge
namespace NearCoreGapSplitEvaluation

